async function consumeEvents(url: string, onEvent: (e: unknown) => void) {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  const reader = response.body!.getReader();
  const decoder = new TextDecoder();

  for (;;) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    if (done) break;

    const text = decoder.decode(value);
    for (const line of text.split("\n")) {
      if (line) onEvent(JSON.parse(line));
    }
  }
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Three independent defects, all at the same layer — the assumption that a chunk is a record.

1. **HTTP status is never checked.** `fetch()` fulfills for 404 and 500; only transport failures reject. `response.body!` also lies: a `204` or `HEAD`-like response has no body and `getReader()` would throw on `null`. Check `if (!response.ok || !response.body) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`)` before touching the stream, so an HTML error page is never fed to `JSON.parse`.

2. **Chunks are transport boundaries, not line boundaries.** A record can be split across two `read()` calls; splitting per chunk then parsing the truncated tail throws `Unexpected end of JSON input`, and the head of the next record is silently dropped. Keep a `pending` string across iterations: append decoded text, split on `\n`, parse everything except the last element, and carry that last element forward. Flush `pending` after the loop ends.

3. **One decoder, stream mode.** `decode(value)` treats each chunk as a complete UTF-8 byte sequence. An emoji is four bytes and `é` is two; when the boundary falls inside a code point, the partial bytes decode to U+FFFD — the mojibake. Use a single `TextDecoder` created outside the loop and call `decoder.decode(value, { stream: !done })`, which keeps the incomplete bytes buffered; the final call with `done === true` (stream false) flushes them. That final flush is also where the last `pending` line gets completed.

4. **Abandoning the stream.** A `throw` from `JSON.parse` or from `onEvent` escapes the loop with the reader still locked and the connection open. Wrap the loop in `try/finally` and `await reader.cancel()` on the way out — or better, pass an `AbortSignal` and abort the whole request — and decide a per-record policy for malformed lines instead of letting one bad record kill the stream. Backpressure needs no extra code: awaiting each `read()` and processing the chunk before requesting the next one is what keeps memory bounded.

Corrected shape:

```ts
const res = await fetch(url, { signal });
if (!res.ok || !res.body) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);

const reader = res.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let pending = "";
try {
  for (;;) {
    const { done, value } = await reader.read();
    pending += decoder.decode(value, { stream: !done });
    const lines = pending.split("\n");
    pending = lines.pop() ?? "";
    for (const line of lines) if (line) consume(line);
    if (done) break;
  }
  if (pending) consume(pending);
} finally {
  await reader.cancel().catch(() => {});
}
```

Sources: MDN `Response.body` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Response/body) and the Streams API (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Streams_API).

