});

scenarios.modelAnswer:
The code uses `beforeunload` as if it were a reliable save hook, and treats `pagehide` as a teardown when it may mean "the page is going into the back/forward cache and will be resumed".

1. **Symptom 1 — the save never runs.** `beforeunload` is not guaranteed: mobile browsers skip it or fire it when the app is already being frozen, and an in-flight `fetch()` in a dying document is killed. The reliable moment is `visibilitychange` to `hidden`, which fires when the user switches apps, tabs, or backgrounds the browser; `pagehide` is the compatible fallback for real navigations. Save there, and make the save durable and idempotent — a local write (IndexedDB/localStorage) plus a queued sync that a service worker can flush later, keyed by draft id so a double save is harmless. `sendBeacon()` is right for the small analytics payload and belongs in that hidden/pagehide path; it is not a substitute for persisting application state.

2. **Symptom 2 — no prompt because there is no warning.** `beforeunload` shows a confirmation only if the handler calls `preventDefault()` (legacy: sets `returnValue`). This handler does neither; it just saves. The correct pattern is conditional: register the listener only while unsaved data exists, call `preventDefault()` in it, and remove the listener as soon as the draft is saved — an always-on `beforeunload` listener both nags and can cost back/forward-cache eligibility.

3. **Symptom 3 — teardown on a page that is being cached.** When the page enters the back/forward cache, `pagehide` fires with `event.persisted === true`, and the same document is later restored with a `pageshow` event (`persisted === true`, `event.persisted` on pageshow too). Unconditionally calling `stopPolling()` and `cancelAnimationFrame()` there kills the work, and since nothing resumes it, the restored page is dead until a reload. Pause on `pagehide` (regardless of `persisted`) and resume on `pageshow`; when `persisted` is true, skip re-boot logic that would duplicate listeners. The same guard applies to `resumeTransientWork()` in the `visibilitychange` handler — it resumes but nothing pauses.

Rewritten shape:

```js
let dirty = false;

const warn = (event) => { event.preventDefault(); event.returnValue = true; };
function setDirty(next) {
  dirty = next;
  window[next ? "addEventListener" : "removeEventListener"]("beforeunload", warn);
}

function persist() {
  if (!dirty) return;
  saveDraftLocally();                  // durable, idempotent, keyed by id
  navigator.sendBeacon("/analytics", JSON.stringify({ type: "hide" }));
  setDirty(false);
}

document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", () => {
  if (document.visibilityState === "hidden") { persist(); pauseTransientWork(); }
  else resumeTransientWork();
});

window.addEventListener("pagehide", () => {
  persist();
  pauseTransientWork();               // teardown the loop, keep the state
});

window.addEventListener("pageshow", (event) => {
  if (event.persisted) resumeTransientWork();  // restored, not re-booted
});
```

Sources: MDN `visibilitychange` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document/visibilitychange_event), `pagehide` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/pagehide_event), `beforeunload` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/beforeunload_event).
